  "result": "**Partly true:** model builders do use reinforcement learning (RL), which is training by trial and error with a score. But that doesn't mean you need to train a model to get a good assistant. Your mission leaves training and its math out, and with three hours a week and a small budget, I'd keep it that way.\n\nHere is the core idea of PPO (Proximal Policy Optimization), a common RL method.\n\n**Example:** your model handles a booking request correctly with probability 0.5. It gets one right, scoring better than expected. An update would raise that probability to 0.7.\n\nPPO training tries to raise the average of:\n\n`min( r × A, clip(r, 1−ε, 1+ε) × A )`\n\n- **r** is new probability ÷ old: 0.7 / 0.5 = 1.4.\n- **A**, the advantage, is how much better than expected the attempt did: say +1.\n- **ε** limits the change: say 0.2. `clip` keeps r between 0.8 and 1.2.\n\nSo you get min(1.4, 1.2) = **1.2**. Pushing past 0.6 probability (r = 1.2) earns nothing extra, which discourages big jumps from limited evidence.\n\nTo train with it, you'd need a model whose weights (internal numbers) you can change, suitable hardware, many example requests and a scoring rule. Then you repeat: attempt, score, estimate A, adjust the weights.\n\nThis is an intuition, **not a derivation**. That needs probability, gradients and neural networks first. See [OpenAI's Spinning Up](https://spinningup.openai.com/en/latest/algorithms/ppo.html), where I checked the formula, and the [original paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.06347).\n\n**Your next step:** write 20 realistic student requests, each with the correct action. This test set will let you measure an already-trained model and see what actually fails. I can make that a short lesson.\n\nDo you want to keep your mission as it is (my recommendation), or change it to include training?",
